  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA broad area of 596dam high pressure at H500 will be present\nover the south and Appalachia through most of the week. This is\nconsidered an exceptionally strong area of high pressure that\nwill act as a heat dome through the week.\n\nMost of the forecast area will remain dry through at least\nWednesday, but passing shortwaves in the upper level ridge\npattern may result in the chance for isolated showers or\nthunderstorms each afternoon this week, mostly across the\nmountains and northern WV. The bubble of high pressure will\nfinally breakdown by Thursday allowing for a more 'dirty ridge'\npattern to setup with a cold front sinking into our area from\nthe north. Areawide chances for showers and thunderstorms return\nThursday and Friday as a result.\n\nTemperatures will be in the upper 80s and lower 90s today across\nthe lowlands. These temperatures will build into mid week with\nlower to upper 90s being common Tuesday through Thursday. Dew\npoints in the upper 60s to lower 70s will create dangerous heat\nindices in the upper 90s and lower 100s, raising concerns for\nheat advisory conditions. Temperatures will moderate some\nstarting Friday into next weekend with a front pushing through\nthe area.",
